#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203662 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1e3b9100-91b5-59ec-8676-832893b05ebb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using the Distributive Laws"}] :question/id #uuid "f6acf09c-f96b-5fb2-9ab0-a12836169173" :question/math-academy-id "e-14746" :question/problem "Find $(-2\\mathbf{a}+5\\mathbf{b}) \\times \\mathbf{c}$, if $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c} = \\mathbf{i}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} = -3\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n$(a ± b) \\cdot c|= a \\cdot c ± b \\cdot c; c \\cdot (a ± b)|= c \\cdot a ± c \\cdot b$\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n$(λa) \\cdot b|= λ(a \\cdot b); a \\cdot (λb)|= λ(a \\cdot b)$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(-2a + 5b) \\cdot c &= (-2a) \\cdot c + (5b) \\cdot c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n$(-2a) \\cdot c + (5b) \\cdot c|=-2(a \\cdot c) + 5(b \\cdot c)$\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2(a \\cdot c) + 5(b \\cdot c) &= -2 \\cdot (i) + 5 \\cdot (-3i + j) \\\\\n&=-2i - 15i + 5j \\\\\n&=-17i + 5j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203663 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88c81beb-9b15-56ae-bb46-ce5cbc1d0ea5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using Anticommutativity"}] :question/id #uuid "0c6d5a4b-e3bd-5637-8fee-e6caeb862d03" :question/math-academy-id "e-14747" :question/problem "Find $2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})$, if $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-2 \\mathbf{k}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n$(a ± b) \\cdot c|= a \\cdot c ± b \\cdot c; c \\cdot (a ± b)|= c \\cdot a ± c \\cdot b$\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n$(λa) \\cdot b|= λ(a \\cdot b); a \\cdot (λb)|= λ(a \\cdot b)$\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n$\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}$.\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n$a \\cdot b|=-b \\cdot a$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n$2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})=(2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (\\mathbf{a}) + (2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3\\mathbf{b})$\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n$(2a) \\cdot (a) + (2a) \\cdot (3b)|= 2(a \\cdot a) + (2 \\cdot 3)(a \\cdot b); = 2(a \\cdot a) + 6(a \\cdot b)$\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}{:}$\n$2(a \\cdot a) + 6(a \\cdot b)|= 2(0) + 6(-b \\cdot a); =-6(b \\cdot a)$\n- Finally, we substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n$-6(b \\cdot a)|=-6(i - j - 2k); =-6i + 6j + 12k$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "88c81beb-9b15-56ae-bb46-ce5cbc1d0ea5" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Cross Product Expressions Using Anticommutativity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a07f440e-d92c-5d61-b1eb-8abaf43656f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 14, 7,-42⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199614} {:answer/id #uuid "b8dbce2e-3443-54f4-862d-7594e3c60f29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨14,-7, 42⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199615} {:answer/id #uuid "ba19a959-3547-54ee-9c91-ab996ba5e8bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨ - 42, 7,-14⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186199616} 